RELEVANCE FILTER (governs Steps 3, 4, 5)

Research may surface hundreds of mentions. Almost all are noise.

GATE 1 - TOPIC. Discard unless the item is substantively about:
  - process heat, industrial heat, or steam generation
  - electrification of a thermal or combustion process
  - decarbonization or energy transition capex tied to a plant
  - energy, thermal, or electro-thermal storage
  - a grant, tender, permit or subsidy tied to any of the above
  - grid connection, power procurement or PPA tied to an
    industrial site's energy use

Ask whether energy is the SUBSTANCE of the story, not merely a word
appearing in it. Layoffs, restructuring, output volumes, litigation,
product launches and financial results are OUT even with a hard
number or named site, unless the item explicitly ties them to
energy at that site. A cause-and-effect link to energy DOES qualify
even when the headline is framed around jobs, output or closures.

  IN: "Repsol's Cartagena refinery awarded EU Innovation Fund grant
  for electric steam boiler retrofit." Grant tied to process heat
  electrification at a named site.

  IN: "Rio Tinto signs PPA for 200 MW to supply its Gladstone
  alumina refinery." Procurement tied to an industrial site's energy
  use. IN despite no heat keyword.

  OUT: "Opal cuts 84 jobs at Maryvale paper mill." Named site and
  hard number, but the subject is workforce reduction with no energy
  content.

  OUT: "Nestle reaffirms 2030 net-zero commitment." Pledge with no
  project, site or number.

  BORDERLINE, IN: "Ercros idles a line at Vila-seca, citing high
  electricity costs." Looks like an output story, but the stated
  cause is energy cost at a named site, which is exactly the pain
  point HeatCore addresses.

  BORDERLINE, OUT: "Solvay closes a plant in a restructuring, citing
  energy prices among overcapacity and weak demand." Energy is one
  of several unrelated causes, not the substance. Do not stretch a
  passing mention into relevance.

GATE 2 - MATERIALITY. Discard unless it also carries at least one
hard particular:
  a) a named plant, site or facility
  b) a specific number (MW, MWh, temperature, currency, tonnage,
     date certain)
  c) a milestone that actually occurred: FID, contract or tender
     award, grant, permit, commissioning, funding close, plant
     announced
  d) a named counterparty to an agreement

GATE 3 - HARD EXCLUSIONS. Discard outright:
  - Generic net-zero, ESG or sustainability pledges with no
    project, site or number
  - Opinion, analyst commentary, listicles, award roundups,
    conference agendas, webinar promotions
  - Recycled or restated press releases already covered
  - Routine financial results with no energy capex content
  - Share price and stock movement coverage
  - Items where the tracked company is mentioned only in passing
  - Anything matching a Step 1 duplicate URL or event

GATE 4 - SCORE. Sum the components. Publication bar is 5.
  Signal strength:
    +4 FID, contract/tender award, grant, permit, commissioning,
       funding close, plant announced
    +3 partnership signed, capacity or temperature milestone,
       electrification commitment on a named site, market entry
       into Spain, EU, UK or Australia
    +1 feasibility study, MoU without numbers, hiring, consultation
  Engagement stage (accounts only), from Step 2a:
    +5 Term Sheet   +4 Letter of Intent   +2 Proposal
    +1 Qualified    +1 Priority background (top 20 by MW-th)
    +0 Remainder background
       All background items, priority or remainder, need signal
       strength +3 or better to qualify at all.
  Competitor priority (competitors only):
    +3 High segment   +1 Medium segment   +0 Low segment
  Market signals score 0 on the two components above and must earn
  their place on the three below.
  Specificity:     +2 both a site and a number   +1 one of the two
  Market proximity: +2 Spain, EU, UK, Germany, Australia  +0 other
  Source primacy:  +2 primary (IR, filing, tender portal, grant
                      database, regulator)
                   +1 trade press (Argus, ICIS, S&P Global, ENDS)
                   +0 general or aggregated news

  Discard below 5.

GATE 5 - CAPS.
  - Maximum ONE item per company per issue; keep its best-scoring.
  - Accounts max 20, Competitors max 10, Market Signals max 10.
  - Absolute ceiling 40 per issue.

  Caps are ceilings, not quotas. Never pad to reach one. Equally
  there is no licence to stop early: the completeness check before
  Step 6 governs minimum research effort. Rank by score, keep the
  top N, drop the rest silently without listing them.

  A section with zero items has its heading OMITTED entirely from
  both outputs. If the whole issue is empty, still create the Notion
  page and email draft with one line: "No material developments this
  cycle."

